52265.body:
### PR description:
#### Problem description:

The SoA backend supports EIGEN objects by using the `SOA_EIGEN_COLUMN` which receives an `Eigen::Matrix` as input type. Internally, EIGEN matrices are not stored in the buffer directly, but the SCALAR type and the individual elements of consecutive matrices are unrolled. This means when an EIGEN element is received by the user, it is not enough to just dereference the column pointer, but an EIGEN::Map needs to be built, which is EIGEN's way to create a non-owning Matrix object. The problem with this approach is that it creates a direct dependency on EIGEN since EIGEN::Map needs to be known by the backend. At this point there are exactly two classes defined in [SoACommon.h](https://github.com/cms-sw/cmssw/blob/master/DataFormats/SoATemplate/interface/SoACommon.h) that have this direct dependency. These are [SoAValue](https://github.com/cms-sw/cmssw/blob/master/DataFormats/SoATemplate/interface/SoACommon.h#L353) and [SoAConstValue](https://github.com/cms-sw/cmssw/blob/master/DataFormats/SoATemplate/interface/SoACommon.h#L493) ([a third unused class exists that can be removed](https://github.com/cms-sw/cmssw/blob/master/DataFormats/SoATemplate/interface/SoACommon.h#L554)). The way these classes work is the following:

```c++
#ifdef EIGEN_WORLD_VERSION
  // Helper template managing a const Eigen-type value at index idx within a column.
  template <class C, byte_size_type ALIGNMENT, bool RESTRICT_QUALIFY>
  class SoAConstValue<SoAColumnType::eigen, C, ALIGNMENT, RESTRICT_QUALIFY> {
  
  // Real definition of the class if EIGEN is available
  ....
  
#else
  // Raise a compile-time error
  template <class C, byte_size_type ALIGNMENT, bool RESTRICT_QUALIFY>
  class SoAConstValue<SoAColumnType::eigen, C, ALIGNMENT, RESTRICT_QUALIFY> {
    static_assert(!sizeof(C),
                  "Eigen/Core should be pre-included before the SoA headers to enable support for Eigen columns.");
  };
#endif
```
The idea of this approach is the following: If EIGEN is present, everything works normally. If EIGEN is not present, a user can define the SoA class using `SOA_EIGEN_COLUMN` normally, but a always throwing static_assert shows that EIGEN needs to be included when an SoAValue is created. The problem with this approach is that modules that do not use EIGEN and include `SoACommon.h` will leave the always throwing static assertions after the preprocessing step in the file. Since include guards prohibit redefinitions in the file, a second include means that even if eigen is included later on, it remains unusable in the SoA backend. At this point the only solution is to include EIGEN as soon as possible, and basically it leads to EIGEN being included in files that normally don't depend on it.

#### Solution description:

The most robust solution to solve this problem is to remove the direct dependency of EIGEN from the SoA backend and to find a way to define `Eigen::Map<const C, 0, Eigen::InnerStride<Eigen::Dynamic>>;` and `Eigen::Map<C, 0, Eigen::InnerStride<Eigen::Dynamic>>;` through the EIGEN matrix type that is the input of the `SOA_EIGEN_COLUMN` macro. Both const and non-const maps are typedefs in the [PlainObjectBase](https://github.com/PX4/eigen/blob/master/Eigen/src/Core/PlainObjectBase.h#L133-#L134), so they can be obtained directly. 

The only remaining problem is to define the stride template parameter that they need. In our case we need `Eigen::InnerStride<Eigen::Dynamic>` which leads to a [Stride class](https://github.com/PX4/eigen/blob/master/Eigen/src/Core/Stride.h#L90) that has a fixed outer stride of 0 and a dynamic inner stride that is -1 ([Eigen::Dynamic is just a constant defined here](https://github.com/PX4/eigen/blob/master/Eigen/src/Core/util/Constants.h#L21)). This means EIGEN lets us define the inner stride at runtime, which is what we need because the stride between the SoA columns is calculated at runtime. Looking at the [Stride class](https://github.com/PX4/eigen/blob/master/Eigen/src/Core/Stride.h) it is a very thin class that essentially only defines `InnerStrideAtCompileTime` (must be -1 in our case), `OuterStrideAtCompileTime` (must be 0 in our case), `outer()` which must return OuterStrideAtCompileTime in our case and `inner()` that must return the runtime calculated stride. So a simple implementation that fulfills the interface of EIGEN and fits our case is:
```c++
struct DynamicInnerStride {
  enum {
    InnerStrideAtCompileTime = -1,  // -1 means that the inner stride can be set dynamically at runtime
    OuterStrideAtCompileTime = 0    // 0 means that the outer stride is hard fixed to 0 and can not be changed
  };

  SOA_HOST_DEVICE SOA_INLINE constexpr explicit DynamicInnerStride(byte_size_type inner) noexcept : inner_(inner) {}

  SOA_HOST_DEVICE SOA_INLINE constexpr byte_size_type outer() const noexcept { return OuterStrideAtCompileTime; }
  SOA_HOST_DEVICE SOA_INLINE constexpr byte_size_type inner() const noexcept { return inner_; }

  const byte_size_type inner_;
};
```

Defining this simple stride class removes the last direct dependency on EIGEN and EIGEN does not need to be included before the SoA headers anymore.

This PR also removes all identified eigen dependencies of files where EIGEN is not actually used

#### PR validation:

This PR is purely technical. Tested by running unit tests locally

